text:
He sang as they were passing through the town,

With an open song and wild melody,—

“Love is like wine. It comes before you know it;

Oh! I feel that the years must be falling fast—
We are drifting into old age as we drift down;
We know not where the sands shall run out at last;
No one can tell if the spring will ever come again.

I love a flower that blows;
   Just so much as I love you ;
And if it dies tomorrow,
   All things are changed for me too.

I sat at dinner in the dining-room,
My eyes were fixed upon that book alone;
And I heard not what they talked around me;
But when I asked for wine, then did they shout
Aloud: 'It is come! He will drink no more.'

How many are sick in prison, or do die there;

For want of food, of medicines, or remedy;

The prisons being foul, pestilential and loathsome,

Foul as the holes where wild beasts dwell and live!

And some prisoners eat nothing for days together.

How often is their water-supply cut off,

Or otherwise polluted, and they forced to drink it;

So that all who touch it or taste thereof, will be sick!

I had one idea in mind, but then I changed it;
 For I thought perhaps another might be better.
 The one idea was my own, and I made no mistake;
 But what could have happened, I do not pretend to say.

The wind upon the air;

The sun's soft beam;
The rain descending fair;
Or, still more dear—

IN all that summer scene, so fair,
 I saw not one who seemed aware
 Of what my soul had learned and loved;

I sat on a wall that was wet with dew;
It gleamed white against the purple sky;
And I could see all the fields around me;
The hayfields, where the cattle lay asleep,
While round them the air went softly hummed,
As if it were dreaming too of rest,
After the busy heat of summer time.
